The existing white light-emitting diodes (WLEDs) are mostly fabricated with downshifting phosphors and ul­ traviolet (UV) or blue light LED chips. To achieve unconventional upconversion white-light-emitting phosphors, a novel strategy by encapsulating upconversion nanoparticles and organic dyes into metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) at room temperature is put forward. Herein, lanthanide-doped upconversion nanoparticles $\mathrm{(NaYF_{4};Y b}$ , $\mathrm{Tm}@\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}$ core–shell UCNPs), dye 4-(dicyanomethylene)-2-methyl-6-(p-dimethylaminostyryl)-4H-pyran (DCM) and dye Coumarin 6 (C6) with blue, red and green light emission, respectively, are encapsulated together into ZIF-8 through a facile one-pot solid-confinement conversion process. Owing to their appropriate spectral overlaps and efficient energy transfer processes between two pairs of UCNPs/DCM and $\mathrm{UCNPs/C6}$ within ZIF-8, the optimized UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film exhibits high-quality white-light emission upon a commercial $980~\mathrm{nm}$ LED chip with ideal CIE coordinates of (0.33, 0.33), high color rendering index (CRI) value of 93 and moderate correlated color temperature (CCT) value of 5567 K. This strategy paves the way for other upcon­ version phosphorous nanoparticles and fluorescent molecules for WLEDs applications excited by near infrared light.
## 1. Introduction
In recent decades, solid-state lighting (SSL) has become a very important element of human life [1–3],[4]. Among them, white-lightemitting diodes (WLEDs) are regarded as the most promising solidstate lighting sources owing to their high luminous efficiency, pro­ longed lifetime, low power consumption and environmental friendliness [5–8]. Up to now, three general methods have been designed to achieve WLEDs. The first approach is the direct combination of blue, green and red LED chips. Nevertheless, the different degradation rate of each chip and the complex fabrication process restrict their extensive application [9,10]. A second approach includes an ultraviolet (UV) or violet LED chip combined with phosphors that converts UV or violet light into a broadband white light [11]. Compared to the first, color-conversion WLEDs without direct charge injection are more easily achieved. But the downshifting of UV or violet light undergoes a low efficiency and the release of UV radiation is harmful to human health [12,13]. The last and most popular approach based on the partial conversion concept is the integration of a blue LED chip and phosphors emitting yellow light. A part of blue light is converted by phosphors and the rest transmitted light can be mixed by yellow light to produce white light. However, individual phosphors and chips exhibit different degradation rates, resulting in poor white light performance [14,15]. Thus, designing a single-phase phosphor with white light emission upon the LED chip excitation is very important for the development of promising WLEDs.
Upconversion materials, which can convert near-infrared (NIR) excitation into visible-wavelength emission through the step-wise ab­ sorption of multiple photons, have attracted much interest due to their wide applications in 3-dimensional displays [16], optically-pumped la­ sers [17], night vision as well as biological imaging [18,19],[20]. Among the existing upconversion materials, lanthanide-doped upcon­ version nanoparticles (UCNPs) stands out for its specific line-like emis­ sion peaks [21], uninterrupted emission ability [22], and remarkable photo stability [23,24]. Recently, we designed $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ co-doped
UCNPs with broad tunable lifetime, which emitted blue light at around $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ upon $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation [25–28]. It is well known that the current WLEDs are mostly fabricated with downshifting phosphors and an UV, violet or blue light LED chip [6],. If they can be prepared with upconversion nanoparticles and near-infrared light LED chips, it would be a novel strategy for WLEDs fabrication based on upconversion pro­ cess instead of traditional downshifting process. Lately, many whitelight-emitting upconversion nanostructures have been developed, but most of which can only be excited by $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser power [29–32]. To achieve white light emission under infrared light LED excitation, we prepared $\mathrm{NaYF_{4}.Y b,T m}@\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}$ core–shell UCNPs emitting strong blue light upon $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation, which is possible to obtain white light according to additive color mixing principle by introducing green- and red-emitting dye molecules, such as coumarin derivatives (C6) and 4- (dicyanomethylene)-2-methyl-6-(p-dimethyla-minostyryl)-4H-pyran (DCM). Notably, these two dye molecules can be excited by $470\mathrm{nm}$ light at the same time, so they can emit green and red light through the energy transfer from UCNPs to produce white light [33],.
Up to now, in order to improve the white light performance of WLEDs, various techniques embedding phosphors into different matrix have been developed [34–37]. Recently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) assembled from metal ions and ligands have been explored as ideal matrix for dye encapsulation due to their tunable microporous structures [38,39], high porosities and surface area [40], as well as remarkable thermal stability [41,42]. Nevertheless, most MOFs crystals are in form of powders, requiring additional blending and coating pro­ cesses for white-light-emitting LED chips [43–45]. Therefore, it is urgent to obtain MOF-based film-like WLEDs with high-quality white light emission. Among thousands kinds of MOFs, ZIF-8 with well-defined cavities and high thermal stability is very suitable for dye encapsula­ tion, due to its excellent chemical stability [46,47], ordered micro­ structure and high transparency in the visible region [48,49]. As discussed above, to achieve single phase white-light-emitting thin films upon near-infrared excitation, UCNPs, green- and red-emitting dyes need to be confined in ZIF-8 thin films simultaneously.
In this work, we report a novel strategy for film-like near-infraredexcitable WLEDs fabrication by encapsulating $\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}\mathrm{:Yb,Tm}@\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}$ UCNPs, DCM and C6 into ZIF-8 (UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8) at room tem­ perature through a facile one-pot solid-confinement conversion process [48,49]. Positively charged nanofibrous zinc hydroxide nanostrands (ZHNs) were used as precursor, which not only serve as zinc source to produce ZIF-8 thin films by reacting with 2-Methylimidazole (Hmim), but also firmly confine UCNPs, DCM and C6 molecules through elec­ trostatic interaction. As a result, UCNPs, DCM and C6 molecules are well isolated and uniformly distributed in ZIF-8 matrix. Upon near-infrared light $\mathsf{(980~n m)}$ excitation, UCNPs embedded in ZIF-8 emit strong blue light based on upconversion process, DCM as well as C6 dyes emit strong red and green light simultaneously through efficient energy transfer process. After optimization, upon $980\mathrm{nm}$ light excitation, the resulting film exhibits high-quality white-light emission with ideal CIE co­ ordinates of (0.33, 0.33), high CRI of about 93 and moderate CCT value of 5567 K. Furthermore, UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 demonstrates excellent thermal stability and long-term durability in air. White light emission is directly achieved by assembling the thin film onto a commercial $980\mathrm{nm}$ LED chip, thus opening a new perspective for the development of WLEDs excited by near infrared light.
## 2. Material and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
$\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , hydrochloric acid and ethanol were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. ltd. Ethanolamine (AE) was purchased from Acros Chemicals. Coumarin 6 (C6), 4-(dicyanomethylene)-2- methyl-6-(p-dimethylaminostyryl)-4H-pyran (DCM) and 2-Methylimi­ dazole $\mathrm{{(Hmim)}}$ were purchased from aladdin.
## 2.2. Material characterization
The morphological features and internal structures of UCNP $@$ ZIF-8, UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8, UCNP/C6@ZIF-8, UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 were observed using field emission scanning electronic microscopy (SEM) (Hitachi S-4800) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Tecnai G2 F20 S-TWIN). The crystalline structures of the above membranes were recorded by an X’Pert PRO (SHIMADZU XRD-6000) instrument with Cu $\mathrm{K}\upalpha$ radiation in the 2θ range of $5^{\circ}-40^{\circ}$ . The PL spectra of the UCNPs solution, UCNP $@$ ZIF-8, UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8, UCNP/C6@ZIF-8, UCNP/DCM/C6 $@$ ZIF-8, DCM and C6 in DMF solution were measured by spectrophotometer (Edinburgh Instruments FLS920) with a $980~\mathrm{nm}$ laser. The ultraviolet/visible absorption spectra of DCM and C6 in DMF solution were conducted on UV/Vis spectrometer (Agilent Cary 5000). The FTIR spectra of ZIF-8, UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 and C6 are recorded by FTIR TENSOR 27 equipment using a KBr pellet. The Zeta potential of UCNPs dilute solution was measured by Zetasizer Nano-ZS equipment. The surface roughness of the UCNP/DCM/C6 $@$ ZIF-8 thin film was recorded on MultiMode equipment from VEECO in America.
## 2.3. Synthesis of ZHNs
The synthesis of ZHNs was based on our previous experiments [48,49]. 8 mM $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ ethanol/water (volume 2:3) solution was mixed with $1.6~\mathrm{mM}$ AE ethanol/water (volume 2:3) solution quickly. The obtained mixture was stirred for $5\mathrm{min}$ and then sealed and stored at room temperature for about $30\mathrm{min}$ .
## 2.4. Synthesis of ligand-free $N a Y F_{4}$ :Yb/Tm@NaYF4 UCNPs
$\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}\mathrm{:Yb/Tm}$ nanoparticles was synthesized by a conventional coprecipitation method [27]. Typically, $2\ \mathrm{mL}\ \mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{Ac})_{3}\ (0.2\ \mathrm{M},\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Y},$ Yb, Tm) aqueous solution was added into $3~\mathrm{mL}$ oleic acid and 7 mL 1- octadecene mixed solution. The mixture was heated at $130^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to remove the water and then heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooling down to $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , 6 mL NaOH (1 mM) and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ $(1.6\mathrm{mM})$ methanol solution was added into the mixture and the resulting mixed solution was stirred for 30 mins. When methanol evaporated completely, the mixed solution was heated to $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under nitrogen for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ and then cooled down to room temperature. The resulting products were precipitated by adding ethanol, collected by centrifugation for several times, and finally dispersed in cyclohexane. The $\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}$ shell precursor was prepared by mixing 2 mL $\Upsilon(\mathrm{Ac})_{3}$ $(0.2~\mathrm{M})$ , $3~\mathrm{mL}$ oleic acid and $7~\mathrm{mL}$ 1-Octadecene aqueous solution in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ flask and subsequently heated at $130^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to remove water contained in the mixed solution, and then heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 h before cooling to $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . $\mathrm{\DeltaNaYF_{4}}$ :Yb/Tm core nanoparticles in cyclohexane was added along with $6~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol solution of NaOH $\left(1\mathrm{mM}\right)$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ ( $\mathrm{~\widetilde{1.6~mM})}$ . The resulting mixture was stirred at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 30 mins. After methanol was evaporated, the mixture was heated to $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under nitrogen for $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . The products were precipitated by adding ethanol, collected by centrifugation for several times, and finally dispersed in $4~\mathrm{mL}$ cyclohexane. The ligand-free $\mathrm{NaYF_{4}.Y b/T m}@\mathrm{NaYF_{4}}$ UCNPs were prepared by adding ethanol into $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ cyclohexane dispersion and then redispersed in $1.2~\mathrm{mL}$ HCl solution $(0.1\ \mathrm{~M~}\$ in deionized water). The slurry was sonicated at room temperature for 30 mins and stewed for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Afterwards, the nanoparticles were collected via centrifugation at $20000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ , washed twice with deion­ ized water, and redispersed in $1\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water.
## 2.5. Synthesis of UCNP@ZIF-8, UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8, UCNP/C6@ZIF-8
According to our previous method [48,49], we synthesized $\operatorname{UCNP}@\operatorname{ZIF}.8$ membranes by encapsulating UCNPs into ZIF-8 at room temperature through a one-pot solid-confinement conversion process. In order to prepare UCNP $@$ ZIF-8 membranes named as S1 to S4 with different $\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}{:}\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ UCNPs content, different volumes (0.01 mL, $0.02\ \mathrm{mL}$ , $0.05~\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ ) (see details in Table S1) of UCNPs so­ lution $(3\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}})$ were added into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ prepared ZHNs and the mixtures were stirred for about $5\mathrm{min}$ . After filtering these composites onto a PC membrane $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ pores), UCNPs/ZHNs thin films were obtained and then transferred onto a quartz plate by ethanol-assistant detachment. Eventually, the composite thin films were immersed into a $25~\mathrm{mM}$ 2-Methylimidazole (Hmim) ethanol/water solution (volume ratio 1:4) for $24\mathrm{h}$ and washed them with ethanol and then dried them in air. UCNP/DCM $\scriptstyle{\mathcal{Q}}Z\operatorname{IF}-8$ and $\mathrm{UCNP/C6@ZIF{-}}8$ thin films were synthe­ sized through the same one-pot solid-confinement conversion process. Different volumes of $0.5~\mathrm{mg~mL}^{-1}$ DCM $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\mathrm{70.05~mL}}$ , $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.3~\mathrm{mL}$ , 0.5 mL) or $0.45~\mathrm{mg~mL}^{-1}$ C6 solution $\mathrm{70.2~mL}$ , $0.4~\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.6~\mathrm{mL}$ , $\begin{array}{r}{0.8\ \mathrm{mL}.}\end{array}$ (Table S1) and 0.1 mL UCNPs solution $(3\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ were together added into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ prepared ZHNs and the mixtures were stirred for about 5 min.
## 2.6. Synthesis of optimized UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film
The optimized resulting UCNP/DCM/C6 $@$ ZIF-8 thin film (S-13) on a quartz plate was prepared from $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ZHNs, $0.5\mathrm{mL}$ UCNPs solution (3 $\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ), $0.05\mathrm{mL}$ DCM solution $(0.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ ) and $0.4\mathrm{mL}$ C6 solution $(0.45~\mathrm{mg~mL}^{-1})$ ) with the similar strategy described above [48,49].
## 2.7. Thermal stability and room temperature durability measurements
UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin films (S-13), DCM solution $(0.5~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{L}^{-1}\mathbf{\bar{\Phi}}.$ ) and C6 solution $(0.45\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ ) were heated in air at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 3, 6 and $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. The PL spectra of the corresponding UCNP/ DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin films, DCM solution and C6 solution were measured by spectrophotometer (Edinburgh Instruments FLS920) with a $980~\mathrm{nm}$ laser. In addition, the UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film was directly exposed in air under ambient conditions (room temperature, humidity from 30 to $70\%$ ) for 6 months. The PL spectra of the resulting UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film were recorded by spectrophotometer (Edinburgh Instruments FLS-920) with a $980\mathrm{nm}$ laser.
## 2.8. White light emission demonstration
The optimized UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film (S-13) on a quartz
plate was placed on a commercial near-infrared $980\mathrm{nm}$ LED chip.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structures and photoluminescence properties of UCNP@ZIF-8
The $\mathrm{\DeltaNaYF_{4}}$ :Yb,Tm (30, $0.5\mathrm{\mol^{\circ}}/\mathrm{0})@\mathrm{Na}\mathrm{YF}_{4}$ UCNP $@$ ZIF-8 thin film was synthesized through a very simple solid-confinement conversion process by encapsulating UCNPs into ZIF-8 at room temperature (Scheme 1). Based on our previous experiments [48,49], certain volume (such $0.01\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.02\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.05\mathrm{mL}$ or $0.1\mathrm{mL}$ ) (see details in Table S1) of 3 $\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ UCNPs solution was added into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ZHNs, respectively, for series samples. UCNPs with zeta potential of $-4.89~\mathrm{mV}$ were strongly attracted by positively charged ZHNs and firmly coated onto the surface of the nanofibres through electrostatic interaction (Fig. 1d) and then assembled into UCNP/ZHNs composite dispersion. After filtering these composites onto a polycarbonate (PC) membrane $\cdot200\ \mathrm{nm}$ pores), we got an UCNP/ZHNs thin film, in which UCNPs were distributed in ZIF-8 crystals uniformly. Then, the UCNP/ZHNs thin film was transferred onto a quartz plate by ethanol-assistant detachment and immersed into a 25 mM 2-Methylimidazole (Hmim) ethanol/water solution (volume ratio 1:4) for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Eventually, the resultant UCNP $@$ ZIF-8 thin film was ob­ tained by washing the above membrane with ethanol and drying in air.
In order to investigate the effect of UCNPs content on the structure and photoluminescence properties of UCNP $@$ ZIF-8, UCNP $@$ ZIF-8 thin films with different UCNPs content of 1.6, 3.2, 8.2 and $16.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ named as S-1, S-2, S-3 and S-4 (see details in Table S1) were prepared. It is obvious that the surface of UCNP $@$ ZIF-8 thin films are fairly continuous and well intergrown (Fig. 1b, Figure S1a), which is the same as the surface morphology of pure ZIF-8 (Fig. 1a). Interestingly, the grain size of ZIF-8 decreases after UCNPs encapsulation, it is probably because UCNPs mixed into the pre­ cursor generate more ZIF-8 nuclei and produce smaller ZIF-8 crystals. When the UCNPs content increases, the grain size of ZIF-8 crystals de­ creases. The TEM image of UCNP $@$ ZIF-8 (Fig. 1e) shows a regular ZIF-8 crystal clearly and indicates that UCNPs are incorporated into ZIF-8 successfully. The corresponding element mapping image of fluorine further confirms UCNPs are embedded and distributed uniformly in ZIF-8 crystals (Fig. 1g). Besides, the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image suggests that the size of an upconversion nanocrystal is approximately 30 nm (Fig. 1f), which is consistent with the size of UCNPs before incorpo­ ration as described in our previous work [25,26],[27]. Compared to the simulated ZIF-8 XRD pattern presented in Fig. 2a, the XRD peaks of UCNP $\scriptstyle({\mathcal{Q}}Z\operatorname{IF}-8$ with miller indexes of (0 1 1), (0 0 2), (1 1 2), (2 2 2), (0 4 4) and (2 4 4) agree with the ideal ZIF-8, suggesting the encapsulation of UCNPs does not affect the phase of ZIF-8 crystals.
Upon $980~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, UCNP@ZIF-8 thin films dominantly emit blue light at $450~\mathrm{nm}$ and $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2b), which is aligned with pure
UCNPs [25–27]. Other very weak emission peaks at $510\mathrm{nm}$ , $646\mathrm{nm}$ and $700\mathrm{nm}$ also agree with that of UCNPs, so the encapsulation process has no effect on the PL properties of UCNPs. With the increase of UCNPs content, the PL intensity of UCNP $\scriptstyle({\mathcal{Q}}Z[\mathbb{F}-8$ thin film increases simulta­ neously. Notably, when the UCNPs content is doubled from 1.6 to 3.2 wt $\%$ (S-1 to S-2), the PL intensity of the corresponding membrane is doubled as well, indicating there is no aggregation-induced PL quenching [11,44,50]. However, if we continue to increase the content of UCNPs in the UCNP@ZIF-8 composite film up to $17\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ , unknown flocculent precipitates will appear and it is difficult to generate contin­ uous ZIF-8 thin films (Figure S1e). Therefore, we choose the brightest film with highest PL intensity and UCNPs content (S-4) for next exper­ iment, the corresponding CIE point is calculated as (0.15, 0.07) in the range of blue light (Fig. 2f).
## 3.2. Structures and photoluminescence properties of UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8 and UCNP/C6@ZIF-8
## 3.2.1. Structures of UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8 and UCNP/C6@ZIF-8
To produce white-light-emitting thin films, DCM and C6 molecules with red- and green-light emission were introduced into UCNP $@$ ZIF-8, respectively. Following the similar method [48,49], $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ZHNs was mixed with $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ UNCPs solution and different volumes of $0.5~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ DCM ( $\mathrm{.0.05\mL}$ , $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.3~\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) or $0.45~\mathrm{mg~mL}^{-1}$ C6 so­ lution ( $\mathrm{\cdot}0.2\mathrm{mL}$ , 0.4 mL, $0.6\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.8\mathrm{mL}$ ) (Table S1). Likewise, negatively charged UCNPs, DCM and C6 molecules were attracted by positively charged ZHNs and firmly anchored onto ZHNs through electrostatic interaction to form UCNP/DCM/ZHNs and UCNP/C6/ZHNs nanofibres, in which UCNPs, DCM and C6 are evenly distributed and well isolated. After the same experimental procedure as mentioned, we got UCNP/ $\mathrm{DCM}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ with DCM content of 1.4, 2.8, 8.3 and $13.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ named as S-5 to S-8 as well as UCNP/C6@ZIF-8 thin films with C6 content of 4.9, 9.8, 14.8 and $19.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ named as S-9 to S-12. It can be seen that both UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8 and UCNP/C6@ZIF-8 thin films are continu­ ously and dense inter-grown, the same as ZIF-8 and UCNP@ZIF-8 (Figure S1b, c), suggesting the crystal structure of ZIF-8 does not be affected by DCM and C6 molecules. Notably, both the grain sizes of
UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8 and UCNP/C6@ZIF-8 are smaller than that of ZIF-8 and UCNP@ZIF-8, probably because UCNPs, DCM and C6 molecules added into the precursor create more ZIF-8 nuclei. As shown in Fig. 2a, the XRD patterns of these two thin films are in alignment with simulated pure ZIF-8 crystals, further demonstrating that typical ZIF-8 crystals were obtained.
## 3.2.2. Photoluminescence properties and FERT process of UCNP/ DCM@ZIF-8
As is known to all that the DCM solution exhibits a strong red emission around $630~\mathrm{{nm}}$ excited by $470\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $372{\mathrm{~nm}}$ or $352~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light, and it cannot be excited by near-infrared light [33]. Interestingly, compared to UCNP $@$ ZIF-8 (S-4), upon $980\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, PL intensities of UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8 at $646\mathrm{nm}$ and $700\mathrm{nm}$ in red-light region increase significantly, whereas PL intensities of emission peaks at $450~\mathrm{nm}$ and $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ in blue-light region decrease dramatically (Fig. 2c). This might because the potential fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) process between UCNPs and DCM dyes, UCNPs can be excited by $980\mathrm{nm}$ light and transfer energy to DCM dyes. As expected, with the increase of DCM content, the PL intensities of UCNP/DCM $@$ ZIF-8 at $646~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $700\mathrm{nm}$ are accordingly enhanced and that at $450\mathrm{nm}$ as well as $475\mathrm{nm}$ have a relevant decrease. Then we prepared a DCM@ZIF-8 thin film for reference. The resulting thin film emits strong red light at $640\ \mathrm{nm}$ excited by $470\ \mathrm{nm}$ light, but no fluorescence emission is observed upon $980\mathrm{nm}$ excitation (Fig. 3a), indicating UCNPs play an important role in the energy transfer between $980~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ light and DCM molecules. Furthermore, we measured the emission spectrum of UCNPs and the absorption spectrum of DCM. As shown in Fig. 3c, DCM in DMF solution demonstrates strong absorbance in the range of $430{\mathrm{-}}490\ \mathrm{nm}$ , largely overlapping the characteristic emission peaks of UCNPs at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ . It indicates the FRET process between UCNPs and DCM dyes, UCNPs are the donor and DCM dyes are the acceptor.
## 3.2.3. Photoluminescence properties and FERT process of UCNP/C6@ZIF8
In general, C6 in DMF solution shows intense green emission bands centered at about $520\mathrm{nm}$ , the same as DCM, it can also not be excited by near-infrared light [33]. Similar to the phenomena for UCNP/ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{DCM@ZIF-8}}$ thin films, when compared with UCNP@ZIF-8 (S-4), the PL intensity of $\mathrm{UCNP/C6@ZIF{-}}8$ thin films at $535\ \mathrm{nm}$ in green-light region is greatly strengthened, and PL intensities of emission peaks at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $475\mathrm{nm}$ in blue-light region decrease accordingly (Fig. 2d). Slightly different from UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8, when we continue to in­ crease C6 content, the emission peak at $535~\mathrm{nm}$ red shifts to $555~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is consistent with the literature [45]. As a reference, a ${\mathsf{C}}6@{\mathsf{Z I F}}–8$ thin film was synthesized, which emits bright green-light at $535\ \mathrm{nm}$ upon $470~\mathrm{{nm}}$ excitation and no fluorescence upon $980~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation (Fig. 3b). Afterwards, the absorption spectrum of C6 (Fig. 3d) was recorded. C6 dyes in DMF solution shows strong absorption in the range of $435{-}485\mathrm{nm}$ , which also overlaps the emission peaks of UCNPs at 450 nm and $475\mathrm{nm}$ , thus suggesting the FRET process between UCNPs and C6 with the donor of UCNPs and the acceptor of C6. The CIE coordinates for the red-light emission of UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8 (S-8) and the greenlight emission of UCNP/DCM@ZIF-8 (S-12) are calculated as (0.60, 0.29) and (0.22, 0.51), the corresponding CIE points are in the range of red-light region and green-light region, respectively (Fig. 2f).
## 3.3. Structures and photoluminescence properties of optimized UCNP/ DCM/C6@ZIF-8
After optimization, a white-light-emitting UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film (S-13) was prepared from $10\mathrm{mL}$ ZHNs, $0.5\mathrm{mL}$ UCNPs solution $(3~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ , $0.05~\mathrm{mL}$ DCM solution $(0.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ and $0.4~\mathrm{mL}$ C6 so­ lution $(0.45\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}.$ ) with the similar strategy mentioned above [48,49]. As shown in Fig. 1c and Figure S1d, the same morphology as ZIF-8 thin film, the UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 membrane is wellintergrown and dense continuous with thickness of about $3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The cross-section SEM element mapping results show that yttrium element from UCNPs and sulfur element from DCM are evenly distributed throughout the whole composite film, indicating these molecules are well isolated and encapsulated into ZIF-8 crystals (Figure S2) [25–27].
agrees with simulated typical ZIF-8 well, suggesting that the incor­ poration of UCNPs, DCM and C6 dyes has no effect on the phase of ZIF-8 crystals (Fig. 2a). In order to prove the composite film is suitable for white-light-emitting diodes, we also recorded the surface roughness of the composite film. The roughness of UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 is $111\ \mathrm{nm}$ , smaller than many kinds of phosphors, which is suitable for optical de­ vices (Table S2). Figure S3 obviously shows the characteristic emissions at 450, 475, 545, 646, 700 and $743~\mathrm{nm}$ from this UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 membrane under $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation. In contract, we recorded the PL spectrum of UCNP/DCM/C6 solution excited by $980\mathrm{nm}$ light (Figure S3). It is clear that the mixed solution mainly emits blue light at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is the same as UCNPs, so that there is no FRET process between UCNPs and DCM or C6 dyes. On the contrary, after encapsulating these molecules together into the porous ZIF-8, two main energy transfer processes between the two pairs of UCNPs/DCM and UCNPs/C6 occur immediately. This further confirmed by the PL lifetime spectra of the UCNPs and UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 upon $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation (Figure S4). After mixed with DCM and C6 molecules and encapsulated into ZIF-8 together, the luminescence lifetime of UCNPs at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ decreases from $0.32~\mathrm{ms}$ to $0.22~\mathrm{ms},$ indicating the energy transfer between UCNPs and dyes. Thus the encapsulation process of embedding UCNPs, DCM and C6 molecules into ZIF-8 crystals is crucially important. Interestingly, as shown in Figure S5, the absorption of DCM in the range of $430{\mathrm{-}}490\ \mathrm{nm}$ slightly overlaps the emission peak of C6 at $535~\mathrm{nm}$ . Therefore, energy transfer process also takes place between C6 and DCM, in which C6 dyes are the donor and DCM dyes are the acceptor.
## 3.4. Thermal stability, long-term durability and dependence on power density of UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8
Before applied for WLEDs, the UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 membrane requires excellent thermal stability and long-term durability. It has been mentioned that ZIF-8 exhibits high thermal stability up to $823\mathrm{~K~}$ [46–50], which should be a good host for WLEDs. For comparison, the PL spectra of DCM and C6 solution after heated at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 3, 6 and $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ (Fig. 4a) were measured. It can be seen that fluorescence emissions of DCM and C6 in DMF solution are degraded quickly with the increase of heating time. After $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the fluorescence of C6 in DMF solution has already half quenched, and then $75.6\%$ quenches $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ later. The fluo­ rescence quenching of DCM is slower than that of C6. The degradation of DCM emission at $640~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is $34.1\%$ after treated at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and $84.2\%$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ , suggesting C6 and DCM molecules are not stable in DMF solution at high temperature. Then the PL spectra of the optimized thin film (S-13) after heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in air for 3, 6 and $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. 4b) were recorded. Fortunately, the fluorescence of UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film is mostly remained after heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ over different time. The degradation of PL intensity at $646\mathrm{nm}$ is only $2.46\%$ for $3\mathrm{h}$ , $4.75\%$ for 3 h and $7.91\%$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . And this heat treatment does not affect the crystal structure of UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8. Obviously, after heated for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the surface morphological characteristics of the resulting film have no change (Fig. 5), indicating the UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film with good thermal stability is suitable for WLEDs.
Besides, the long-term durability of the optimized membrane (S-13) was investigated by directly placing it in air under ambient conditions (room temperature, humidity from 30 to $70\%$ ) for 6 months. As illustrated in Fig. 4c, the PL intensity of $\mathrm{UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8}$ at $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ suffers $1.21\%$ loss after 1 month, $3.75\%$ after 3 months and $7.14\%$ after 6 months. The degradation rate is only approximately $1.2\%$ per month. Other emission peaks at $543~\mathrm{nm}$ and $646~\mathrm{{nm}}$ slightly degrade after long exposure time as well. The slightly optical degradation of UCNP/DCM/ ${\mathsf{C}}6@{\mathsf{Z I F}}–8$ thin film probably derives from the dyes and UCNPs which anchored on the most out surface of the composite membrane. Therefore, the longtime interaction of these dyes with water, oxygen and dust will influence the energy transfer process between UCNPs and dyes and degrade their PL performance. As shown in Figure S6, the PL intensity of C6 after exposed in air for 3 and 6 months suffered $4.12\%$ loss after 3 months and $8.57\%$ after 6 months. However, after 7 months, the PL in­ tensity of the composite film has not obviously declined further. This might be attributed to the hydrophobic surface and narrow pore size of ZIF-8, which can prohibit water or other gases in air to enter ZIF-8 framework significantly and ensure a favorable environment for UCNPs, DCM and C6 [46–50]. The corresponding CIE coordinates of UCNP/DCM/ ${\mathsf{C}}6@{\mathsf{Z I F}}–8$ after exposed in air for different time are still in white-light region, suggesting the prepared UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film can emit stable white light for a long time (Figure S7a). The SEM image in­ dicates the surface morphology of UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film re­ mains unchanged after 6 months (Figure S1f). At last, the dependence of UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 on power density was investigated. It is clear that when the laser power density increases from $1.45\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ to $7.8\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the PL intensity of UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 increases accordingly and the film does not show significant changes in emission colors (Fig. 4e). From Figure S7b, the CIE points of UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film excited by different power are close to that of ideal white light point (0.33, 0.33). In order to investigate the fundamental upconversion mechanism, we plotted the PL intensities of S-13 for different emission peaks ( $\mathrm{450~nm}$ , $475\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $646~\mathrm{{nm}},$ as a function of pumping power at a log scale (Fig. 4f), which agrees with the relationship between UCNPs and power densities or other upconversion materials and power densities [25–29], indicating UCNPs is the original source of white light upon $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation.
## 3.5. Fabrication of rough WLEDs
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, UCNPs, DCM and C6 are encapsulated into microporous
MOFs ZIF-8 through a one-pot solid-confinement conversion process for novel near-infrared-excitable high-quality white-light-emitting diodes. Upon $980\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, the UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 membrane exhibits white light emission derived from the encapsulated UCNPs, DCM and C6 phosphors. Due to their appropriate spectral overlaps and efficient en­ ergy transfer process between two pairs of UCNPs/DCM and UCNPs/C6, DCM and C6 can emit red and blue light excited by near-infrared light, which is different from their optical properties under normal conditions. Besides, UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 can emit high-quality white light after heated at high temperature for a long time and exposed in air under ambient conditions for 6 months. By assembling the UCNP/DCM/ C6@ZIF-8 thin film onto a commercial $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ near-infrared LED chip, a high-performance WLED lamp with ideal CIE coordinates of (0.33,0.33), moderate CCT of 5567 K and high CRI of 93 is fabricated successfully, thus demonstrating the potential applications for infrared sensor, such as some warning systems and other under-water equip­ ment. Best to our knowledge, the UCNP/DCM/C6@ZIF-8 thin film is the first near-infrared-excitable phosphor with such high-quality white-light emission upon $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ LED excitation among MOF phosphors reported in WLEDs. With our facile solid-confinement conversion, other phos­ phorous nanoparticles and fluorescent molecules can be encapsulated into MOFs to realize high-quality white-light emission, opening a new perspective to develop high-performance up-conversion white-lightemitting diodes.
